WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.140
AI risk assessment and AI system impact assessment are related, but they are not the same process.

2
00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:16.040
A risk assessment often asks how uncertainty affects objectives, operations, security, quality, compliance, or organizational outcomes.

3
00:00:16.140 --> 00:00:27.100
An impact assessment asks more directly how an AI system and its foreseeable uses may affect individuals, groups, communities, organizations, or society.

4
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:33.600
Collapsing both into one generic form can hide the people and contexts most affected by the system.

5
00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:38.680
Imagine a university adopting an AI tool to prioritize student-support outreach.

6
00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:47.580
The risk assessment may examine accuracy, data access, security, supplier change, operational reliance, and escalation.

7
00:00:47.680 --> 00:01:02.160
The impact assessment asks who may be affected, whether some students are missed or over-targeted, whether the process changes trust, whether students can contest a decision, and whether benefits or burdens fall unevenly across groups.

8
00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:06.500
The same technical result can have different significance depending on context.

9
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.740
Impact assessment should begin early enough to influence design and governance.

10
00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:28.640
It may be needed during concept, before material deployment, before significant new use, when affected populations change, when important data sources change, after significant incidents, when monitoring reveals unexpected effects, during major provider changes, and sometimes during retirement.

11
00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:40.340
ISO/IEC 42005:2025 can support this work, but it complements ISO/IEC 42001 rather than replacing the AIMS.

12
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:46.440
The impact record should therefore feed risk treatment, controls, communication, and monitoring.

13
00:01:46.720 --> 00:02:15.520
Useful impact-assessment elements include system purpose, intended use, foreseeable misuse, context of use, affected parties, benefits, adverse effects, severity, scale, duration, reversibility, distribution of benefits and harms, vulnerable or differently affected groups, oversight, contestability, monitoring, mitigation, residual impacts, approval, and review.

14
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:18.180
Stakeholder involvement should be proportionate.

15
00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:27.080
A low-impact internal tool may not require public consultation, while a system affecting access to essential services may require broader input.

16
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.800
The assessment should also look beyond immediate outputs.

17
00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:41.380
Several small systems may influence the same people, automation may shape future data, human skills may erode, incentives may change, or users may over-rely on recommendations.

18
00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:49.320
The organization should understand not only whether the AI system works, but whom it affects, how those effects may change, and when reassessment is necessary.

19
00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:54.780
Some effects may be temporary and reversible; others may persist after a system is retired.

20
00:02:54.880 --> 00:03:05.440
That is why impact assessment should stay connected to monitoring, complaints, recourse, supplier change, and management review rather than living as a one-time launch document.

21
00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:12.100
It should influence who approves deployment, what is watched, and when intervention is expected.
